\begin_layout Subsection
Operação de Matrizes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Very often,
 an 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times n$
\end_inset

-matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 with real entries represents a linear map from the space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 of vectors of length 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 to the space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 of vectors of length 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
 Similarly,
 a matrix with complex entries represents a linear map from 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{C}^{n}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{C}^{m}$
\end_inset

.
 This map is defined by the matrix-vector product.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\series bold
Definition:

\series default
 Given a real (resp.
 complex) 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times n$
\end_inset

-matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 the linear map associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is the map that sends each vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 to the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $A\boldsymbol{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
 each vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{v}\in\mathbb{C}^{n}$
\end_inset

 to the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $A\boldsymbol{v}\in\mathbb{C}^{m}$
\end_inset

).
 :::
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Matrix multiplication is defined so that the product 
\begin_inset Formula $AB$
\end_inset

 represents the linear map gotten by first applying 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 and then 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 In other words,
 we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(AB)\boldsymbol{v}=A(B\boldsymbol{v})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Note that on the left we have one matrix multiplication and one matrix-vector multiplication,
 while on the right we just have two matrix-vector multiplications.
 Commutators play an extremely important role in quantum physics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\series bold
Definition
\series default
:
 Given two 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times n$
\end_inset

-matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 the commutator of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times n$
\end_inset

-matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $[A,B]=AB-BA.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\series bold
Definition:

\series default
 The transpose of an 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times n$
\end_inset

-matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times m$
\end_inset

-matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{\top}$
\end_inset

 defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $(A^{\top})_{i,j}=A_{j,i}.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\series bold
Definition:

\series default
 The adjoint (conjugate transpose or Hermitian transpose) of an 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times n$
\end_inset

-matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times m$
\end_inset

-matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{\dagger}$
\end_inset

 obtained by applying the complex conjugate and the transpose operation:
 
\begin_inset Formula $(A^{\dagger})_{i,j}=\overline{A_{j,i}}.$
\end_inset

 The adjoint of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is also known as the ** or ** of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\series bold
Definition
\series default
:
 The trace of an 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times n$
\end_inset

-matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is the sum of the diagonal entries of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{Tr}\left(A\right)=\sum^{n}_{i=1}A_{i,i}.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The determinant of an 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times n$
\end_inset

-matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 can be defined in various ways.
 We will make use of permutations.
 Using the Einstein summation convention
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Einstein notation is a notational convention that implies summation over a set of indexed terms in a formula,
 thus achieving brevity.
 There are essentially three rules of Einstein summation notation,
 namely:
 1) Repeated indices are implicitly summed over.
 2) Each index can appear at most twice in any term.
 3) Each term must contain identical non-repeated indices (
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Wolfram MathWorld"
target "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EinsteinSummation.html"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and the Levi-Civita symbol
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
More generally,
 in 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 dimensions,
 the Levi-Civita symbol is defined by:
 +1 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(a_{1},\dots,a_{n}\right)$
\end_inset

is an even permutation from 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(1,2,\dots,n\right)$
\end_inset

,
 -1 if is an odd permutation and 0 otherwhise.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 commonly used in physics,
 we can also write this as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\det A=\epsilon_{a_{1}\dots a_{n}}A_{1,a_{1}}\dots A_{n,a_{n}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Properties of the determinant :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A square matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is invertible if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\det(A)$
\end_inset

 is non-zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Given two 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times n$
\end_inset

-matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\det(AB)=(\det A)(\det B).$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The determinant of a diagonal matrix (and more generally of an upper or lower triangular matrix) is the product of the diagonal entries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
4.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is obtained from 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 by scaling a row by a scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\det B=c\det A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
5.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is obtained from 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 by swapping two rows,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\det B=-\det A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
6.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is obtained from 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 by adding a multiple of a row to another row,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\det B=\det A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Properties 4-6 also hold for columns instead of rows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 we define the characteristic polynomial of a square matrix;
 this will be used in diagonalisation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\series bold
Definition
\series default
:
 The characteristic polynomial of an 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times n$
\end_inset

-matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is the polynomial of degree 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 in a variable 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{A}(t)=\det(x\mathbb{I}-A).$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
An alternative definition of the characteristic polynomial,
 which agrees with the one above up to a factor 
\begin_inset Formula $(-1)^{n}$
\end_inset

,
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{A}(t)=\det(A-x\mathbb{I}).$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Frequentemente,
 uma matriz 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 de dimensão 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times n$
\end_inset

 ,
 com entradas reais,
 representa uma transformação linear do espaço 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

,
 formado por vetores de comprimento 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

,
 para o espaço 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

,
 formado por vetores de comprimento 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Analogamente,
 uma matriz com entradas complexas representa uma transforação linear de 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{C}^{n}$
\end_inset

 para 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{C}^{m}$
\end_inset

.
 Essa aplicação é definida pelo produto entre a matriz e o vetor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\series bold
Definição
\series default
:
 Dada uma matriz 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 real (complexa) de dimensão 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times n$
\end_inset

,
 a transformação linear associada a 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 é a transformação que leva cada vetor 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 ao vetor 
\begin_inset Formula $A\boldsymbol{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 ( cada vetor 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{v}\in\mathbb{C}^{n}$
\end_inset

 ao vetor 
\begin_inset Formula $A\boldsymbol{v}\in\mathbb{C}^{m}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A multiplicação de matrizes é definida de modo que o produto 
\begin_inset Formula $AB$
\end_inset

 represente a transformação linear obtida aplicando primeiro 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 e depois 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 Em outras palavras,
 temos
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left(AB\right)\boldsymbol{v}=A\left(B\boldsymbol{v}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Observe que,
 no lado esquerdo,
 temos uma multiplicação de matrizes e uma multiplicação matriz-vetor,
 enquanto,
 no lado direito,
 temos apenas duas multiplicações matriz-vetor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Na física quântica,
 as matrizes são frequentemente quadradas e possuem algumas propriedades adicionais que as tornam adequadas para atuar como operadores sobre um sistema físico ou como observáveis de tal sistema.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Muitas das definições a seguir são especificamente para matrizes quadradas.
 Um primeiro exemplo é o dos comutadores,
 que desempenham um papel extremamente importante na física quântica.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\series bold
Definição
\series default
:
 Dada duas matrizes 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 de dimensões 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times n$
\end_inset

,
 o comutador de 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

e 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 é a matriz 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times n$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[A,B\right]=AB-BA$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\series bold
Definição
\series default
:
 A transposta de uma matriz 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 de dimensão 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times n$
\end_inset

 é a matriz 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{T}$
\end_inset

 de dimensão 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times m$
\end_inset

 definido por 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(A^{T}\right)_{i,j}=A_{j,i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\series bold
Definiçã
\series default
o
\series bold
:

\series default
 A transposa de uma matriz 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 de dimensão 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times n$
\end_inset

 é a matriz 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{T}$
\end_inset

 de dimensão 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times m$
\end_inset

 definido por 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(A^{T}\right)_{i,j}=A_{j,i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Basis transformations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Until now,
 we have been working with one specific coordinate system in which vectors are identified with their coordinate vectors with respect to the standard basis vectors:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{e}_{1}=\begin{pmatrix}1\\
0\\
0\\
\vdots\\
0
\end{pmatrix},\quad\boldsymbol{e}_{2}=\begin{pmatrix}0\\
1\\
0\\
\vdots\\
0
\end{pmatrix},\quad\ldots,\quad\boldsymbol{e}_{n}=\begin{pmatrix}0\\
0\\
0\\
\vdots\\
1
\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In other words,
 a vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{v}=\begin{pmatrix}v_{1}\\
v_{2}\\
\vdots\\
v_{n}
\end{pmatrix}$
\end_inset

 means the same as the corresponding linear combination of the standard basis vectors:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{v}=v_{1}\boldsymbol{e}_{1}+\cdots+v_{n}\boldsymbol{e}_{n}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In many situations,
 it is useful to express vectors in a different coordinate system.
 For this we need the general concept of a basis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\series bold
Definition
\series default
:
 A basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 consists of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $(\boldsymbol{b}_{1},\ldots,\boldsymbol{b}_{n})$
\end_inset

 (or 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \boldsymbol{b}_{i}\right\} ^{n}_{i=1}$
\end_inset

) such that every vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 can be written in exactly one way as a linear combination:
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{v}=c_{1}\boldsymbol{b}_{1}+\cdots+c_{n}\boldsymbol{b}_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 with scalars 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1},\ldots,c_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 These scalars are called the coordinates of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{v}$
\end_inset

 relative to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \boldsymbol{b}_{i}\right\} ^{n}_{i=1}$
\end_inset

.
 The vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\begin{array}{ccc}
c_{1} & \dots & c_{n}\end{array}\right)^{T}$
\end_inset

 is called the coordinate vector of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{v}$
\end_inset

 relative to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \boldsymbol{b}_{i}\right\} ^{n}_{i=1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Alternatively,
 a basis consists of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \boldsymbol{b}_{i}\right\} ^{n}_{i=1}$
\end_inset

 with the following two properties:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
they span the space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
 every vector is a linear combination of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \boldsymbol{b}_{i}\right\} ^{n}_{i=1}$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
they are linearly independent,
 i.e.
 the only way to write the zero vector as a linear combination of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \boldsymbol{b}_{i}\right\} ^{n}_{i=1}$
\end_inset

is as 
\begin_inset Formula $0\boldsymbol{b}_{1}+\cdots+0\boldsymbol{b}_{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A priori,
 we could have defined a basis as a collection of some number (say 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

) of vectors that span the space and are linearly independent.
 However,
 it can be shown that in fact any two bases contain the same number of vectors.
 Since the standard basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 consists of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 vectors,
 the same therefore holds for any basis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose we have two bases of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

,
 say
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B=(\boldsymbol{b}_{1},\ldots,\boldsymbol{b}_{n})\quad\text{and}\quad C=(\boldsymbol{c}_{1},\ldots,\boldsymbol{c}_{n}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\series bold
Definition:

\series default
 The basis transformation matrix (or change-of-basis matrix) from 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 is the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $P=P_{B\to C}$
\end_inset

 such that the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

-th column of 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 contains the coordinates of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{b}_{j}$
\end_inset

 relative to the basis 
\begin_inset Formula $(\boldsymbol{c}_{1},\ldots,\boldsymbol{c}_{n})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 means:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{b}_{j}=\sum^{n}_{i=1}P_{i,j}\boldsymbol{c}_{i}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given a vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}$
\end_inset

,
 we write 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}^{(B)}=(x^{(B)}_{1},\ldots,x^{(B)}_{n})$
\end_inset

 for the coordinate vector of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}$
\end_inset

 with respect to the basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \boldsymbol{b}_{i}\right\} ^{n}_{i=1}$
\end_inset

,
 and we define 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}^{(C)}$
\end_inset

 similarly.
 Then we calculate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{aligned}\boldsymbol{x} & =\sum^{n}_{j=1}x^{(B)}_{j}\boldsymbol{b}_{j}\\
 & =\sum^{n}_{j=1}x^{(B)}_{j}\sum^{n}_{i=1}P_{i,j}\boldsymbol{c}_{i}\\
 & =\sum^{n}_{i=1}\left(\sum^{n}_{j=1}P_{i,j}x^{(B)}_{j}\right)\boldsymbol{c}_{i}\\
 & =\sum^{n}_{i=1}\bigl(P\boldsymbol{x}^{(B)}\bigr)_{i}\boldsymbol{c}_{i},
\end{aligned}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which shows that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{x}^{(C)}=P_{B\to C}\boldsymbol{x}^{(B)}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly,
 given a linear map 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 we write 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{(B)}$
\end_inset

 for the matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 with respect to the basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \boldsymbol{b}_{i}\right\} ^{n}_{i=1}$
\end_inset

,
 and likewise for 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{(C)}$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{(B)}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{(C)}$
\end_inset

 are related by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A^{(C)}=PA^{(B)}P^{-1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Namely,
 consider what happens when we multiply this matrix to a coordinate vector of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}^{\left(C\right)}$
\end_inset

.
 First,
 applying 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{-1}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note que se 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}^{(C)}=P\boldsymbol{x}^{(B)}$
\end_inset

e sendo 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{-1}P=\mathbb{I}$
\end_inset

,
 então 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{-1}\boldsymbol{x}^{(C)}=\boldsymbol{x}^{(B)}$
\end_inset

,
 o seja 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{-1}=P_{C\rightarrow B}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}^{\left(C\right)}$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}^{\left(B\right)}$
\end_inset

.
 Next,
 applying 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{(B)}$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $(A\boldsymbol{x})^{(B)}$
\end_inset

.
 Finally,
 applying 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $(A\boldsymbol{x})^{(C)}$
\end_inset

,
 which is what we wanted to show.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The trace and the determinant are invariant under basis transformation:
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 are square matrices of the same size with 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 invertible,
 then we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Tr}(PAP^{-1})=\text{Tr}\left(A\right)\quad\text{and}\quad\det(PAP^{-1})=\det A.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because the characteristic polynomial is also defined as a determinant,
 it too is invariant under basis transformation.
\end_layout

